Ohle Claussen <ohle.claus...@ds.mpg.de> wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
>> Ohle Claussen <ohle.claus...@ds.mpg.de> wrote:
>>> Michael Deutschmann wrote:
>>>> On Sat, 1 Dec 2007, Ohle Claussen wrote:

>>>>> How is noting down every object identity (and
>>>>> subsequently looking them up again) easier than
>>>>> storing screenshots of levels?

>>>> It's rare that you'll get sucked before you
>>>> notice an MF is on the level.  So you can hit "\"
>>>> and copy everything down at the last minute.

>>> That would, IMO, make the process _definitely_
>>> cheating.

>> I think you've got a basic confusion going on here
>> between what the PC remembers and what the player
>> remembers. NetHack is only interested in messing up
>> what the PC remembers.

>> I fail to understand how "keeping good notes" of
>> _any_ information the game makes readily available
>> to you can constitute "cheating".

> The mindflaying attack is feared _only_ because it
> causes massive amounts of tedious work to be done
> by the player. The effects can be counteracted by
> casting ID for ages, redoing tedious price- and
> buc-identification, or comparing with notes, in
> which case you did the work before the attack. All
> of those cause little additional danger to the
> character, the main effect is that a lot of time
> has to be spent. Thus, players treat (M)MFs as
> much more dangerous than they actually are to the
> character.

Just as Derek Ray treats pudding farming as "much
more dangerous" (to game balance) than it really is
(because most players don't have the patience to do
it in the first place), a problem that is only
"dangerous" to those who don't have patience to
cater for overcoming it in advance is different than
a problem that is dangerous to everyone.

I grant you, most players are impatient, so to most
players, a geno of MMFs seems well worth the scroll.

> I agree that the word "cheating" is a bit out of
> place, but my opinion is that by copying the item
> identifications immediately before an (M)MF
> encounter, the player circumvents a gameplay
> effect in a way not intended by the developers.

NetHack is _rife_ with "not intended by the
developers" opportunities to make the game easier.
Why is one more so objectionable? I'd call taking
that inventory of "what's known" on an "as needed"
basis a clever savings of player time over doing it
in an ongoing "as the information is added" way,
nothing more.

> W[h]ether making a monster scary by giving it the
> ability to make the player suffer is good design
> or not is another matter,

I think "suboptimal play gets punished" is always
pretty good game design, but I don't make my living
designing games, so...?

> but that's the way (M)MFs work right now.

More stuff than you or I could easily count is put
into NetHack purely for the annoyance value; think,
oh, "duncecap" or "loadstone".

>> Taking good notes just sounds like "due diligence"
>> to me, the difference in caring about
>> winning/surviving between searching obsessively and
>> thus never falling into a trap, versus blithely
>> walking into every trap in your path depending on
>> luck and hit points to keep you from expiring.

> Taking those notes that the game doesn't take for
> you, yes.

Not a valid criterion, since in this case "you can't
trust the game to keep those notes reliably" is
precisely the problem which out of band note taking
will overcome. One could, after all, if one had the
photographic memory of my dad, simply _memorize_ the
entire stinking '\' output list, taking no notes at
all but having exactly the same effect. Notes are
for the fallibility of the human mind, not for the
fallibility of the games "memory" due to amnesia.

> The game does track item identities quite
> conveniently, the '\'-command has no other purpose
> than to take some note-taking work off the player.

Do you have any idea how many _years_ I played the
game before I noticed that function? Up to then,
were I less lazy, out of band notes would have been
my only choice.

> MMF attacks erase those notes, by design (good or
> bad). How could it not be a circumvention of
> design intents to duplicate these notes as well?

What part of "circumventing the design intents" is a
metric of playing goodness or badness? The goal is
to WIN THE GAME, not to make the designers feel like
their design is seamless, without blemish, and
perfect. No game is "perfect"; even tic-tac-toe can
end in a drawn game.

>> YANI: combine the "object amnesia" effect with a
>> "randomize B/U/C statue" effect covering the same
>> objects, perhaps exempting objects currently worn.
>> Even with just a small chance (10%, say) of changing
>> the B/U/C status of any particular object, and even
>> with the original random object distribution of
>> B/U/C status as a result, this would make amnesia
>> attacks or scrolls massively more evil.

> So here you're trying to wedge the player-side
> evilness of amnesia back in after allowing for
> note-taking, which mitigates it.

Well, except that the evilness is equally PC-side,
exactly. Since I don't find any brand of note taking
(even by the PC, by using "#name" to identify
objects redundantly) to be "cheating", even
ttyrec-s, I'd like to see the amnesia stuff have
something included that does makes it meaningful
to PC and player even in the face of good note
taking. "You can't trust the item B/U/C status in
your notes for objects whose ID you've forgotten"
seems like exactly the right level of "meaningful"
impact to increase general game evilness.

